# AI-DLC + Superpowers — Environment Variables
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values.
# NEVER commit .env to version control — it is already in .gitignore.
#
# Which variables you need depends on which integrations you enable:
#   - Jira only:            JIRA_URL, JIRA_USERNAME, JIRA_API_TOKEN
#   - Confluence only:      CONFLUENCE_URL, CONFLUENCE_USERNAME, CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN
#   - Both (recommended):   all six below — the same API token works for both
#   - Official Atlassian remote MCP (alternative): ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN only
#
# Get your API token at: https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens
# Tokens do not expire by default but can be revoked. Rotate them if compromised.

# ── Jira ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Full URL of your Atlassian instance (no trailing slash)
JIRA_URL=https://your-org.atlassian.net

# The email address you use to log in to Jira
JIRA_USERNAME=your-email@example.com

# API token (not your account password)
JIRA_API_TOKEN=your-api-token-here

# ── Confluence ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Full URL of your Confluence instance (no trailing slash, include /wiki)
CONFLUENCE_URL=https://your-org.atlassian.net/wiki

# Same email as Jira
CONFLUENCE_USERNAME=your-email@example.com

# Same API token as Jira — one token covers both services
CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN=your-api-token-here

# ── Official Atlassian Remote MCP (alternative to community server) ───────────
# Use this instead of the six variables above if you prefer the official
# Atlassian remote MCP (https://mcp.atlassian.com/v1/sse).
# See extensions/integrations/jira/mcp-config.json for the config snippet.

# ATLASSIAN_API_TOKEN=your-api-token-here

# ── How to load these variables ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Option A — shell (add to ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, or ~/.bash_profile):
#   export JIRA_URL="https://your-org.atlassian.net"
#   export JIRA_USERNAME="your-email@example.com"
#   export JIRA_API_TOKEN="your-api-token-here"
#   ... (repeat for Confluence vars)
#
# Option B — direnv (https://direnv.net/):
#   Copy this file to .env, fill in values, then run: direnv allow
#   direnv will load the vars automatically when you cd into this directory.
#
# Option C — IDE secret store:
#   Kiro, Cursor, and VS Code all support storing secrets in the IDE keychain.
#   Check your IDE's documentation for "environment variables" or "secrets".
#
# Option D — 1Password / Vault / AWS Secrets Manager:
#   Inject secrets at shell startup using your org's secrets manager.
#   Example with 1Password CLI:
#     eval $(op signin)
#     export JIRA_API_TOKEN=$(op read "op://Private/Atlassian/api-token")
